=== other_side_ending ===

{has_gloria_medallion: -> medallion_crossing}
-> unprotected_crossing


= medallion_crossing

The disc is against your chest. Under your shirt, on the cord Gloria tied, the metal warm from your body heat. Martin Aldrich's containment ward. The same symbol system carved into the walls of this cavern, cast in brass by an engineer who spent thirty years underground and understood what the carvings meant.

Gloria said the deep tunnels felt quieter when he wore it.

The portal exhales. The heat hits you. The pressure. The amber light flares white and your skin burns and you hold on. One hand on the wall. The other hand on the disc.

The pause. A second. Maybe less.

The disc vibrates. Not from the portal's rhythm. Against it. A counter-frequency, subtle, pushing back against the thing it was designed to contain.

You step off the edge.

-> the_other_side


= the_other_side

Heat.

Not the heat of the portal's exhale. That was a wave, a thing that hit you and passed. This is constant. This is ambient. The temperature of stone that has been compressed for longer than anything on the surface has existed.

You're standing. You don't remember the crossing. The portal was there and then it wasn't and now you're here and the ground under your feet is solid. Warm stone. Smooth. Shaped by something that wanted the space to be exactly this shape.

The air is different. Not copper, not ozone, not the chemical sharpness of the portal's breath. Something organic. Old. The smell of deep earth, of mineral deposits and slow water and time measured in eras, not years. It fills your lungs and sits there. Heavy. Too much pressure, not too little.

The disc is still vibrating against your chest. Quieter now. A hum against the hum. The containment symbol holding its small circle of resistance around you. You can feel the boundary: a zone of slightly cooler air that extends about a foot from your body in every direction. Beyond that, the full weight of this place pressing in.

{has_portal_mechanics: The shaft extended "beyond available sounding equipment." The foreman called it bottomless. It wasn't a collapse. It opened. You're inside the thing that opened. The other end of the fifty-year exhale.}

{has_symbol_knowledge: The containment carvings in the cavern above were a lock. You're on the other side of the lock. The space the lock was built to seal.}

You look up. Behind you, above you, the portal is a circle of amber light in the ceiling of this place. Fifteen feet across. Pulsing. The exhale comes from here. The breath originates in this space and rises through the shaft and pushes through the portal and fills the cavern and the tunnels and the mine and the town. You're standing at the source.

The hum here isn't a hum. It isn't a frequency or a vibration or a sound that can be measured with instruments. It's just the sound this place makes. The sound of deep stone under pressure, of geological time, of something vast and patient and continuous. It doesn't pulse. It doesn't breathe. It is.

The space is vast. The ceiling, the floor of the world above, curves away from you in every direction. The amber light fills the chamber without casting shadows. Sourceless. Diffuse. There are no walls visible. The stone floor extends into the amber haze and disappears. The scale is wrong. Nothing underground should be this large.

You walk.

-> finding_mara


= finding_mara

You walk for what might be minutes. The stone floor is smooth under your feet. The disc hums against your chest. The boundary of cooler air moves with you, a foot of protection in every direction, and beyond it the heat presses in, constant, patient.

{has_mara_pages: Mara's map showed three levels. The portal at the bottom. But she drew a fourth mark below the portal, a question mark, circled, with the word "SOURCE" in her careful hand. She was already mapping this place before she crossed. She was preparing to come here.}

You see her before you understand what you're seeing.

A figure. Not the Figure. Not the burlap mask, not the mineral crust, not the calcified remnant that stood at the corridor's end every night and led you down. A person. Upright, moving, alive. Wearing field clothes: khaki pants, a canvas jacket, boots. The clothes are dusty, worn, but intact. Not degraded. Not crystallized. Whole.

She's crouched over something. A notebook. Writing.

The source side holds what it takes more completely than the surface lets you see. The Figure in the corridors above was a projection. A signal degraded by distance. Here, at the origin, the signal is clean.

Mara Castillo.

She looks up. She sees you. Her face changes. Not surprise, not relief. Recognition. The expression of someone who has been waiting for a specific thing and has just confirmed it arrived.

She stands.

"You're Order."

Her voice carries in this space. Not echoing. Not distorted. Clear and immediate and close even across the thirty feet between you.

{mara_clues >= 4: You've known for days. Since the motel bathroom mirror, since the handprint in the tunnels, since Mara's pages laid out the timeline and the disappearance and the trail she left for whoever came next. You knew and now you're looking at her and she's real and solid and here.}

{mara_clues >= 2 && mara_clues < 4: The height. The build. The researcher's posture: weight balanced, eyes tracking, documenting even now. The same posture you saw in the Figure every night, stripped of the burlap and the mineral crust and the distance.}

"How long?" you ask.

"I stopped counting after twenty days." She picks up the notebook. Tucks it into her jacket. "The cycles are consistent. I've been monitoring the exhale patterns. Seventeen-second cycles when I arrived. They've been compressing." She pauses. "What's the date?"

You tell her.

She does the math. You watch her do it. The number lands and she absorbs it without flinching.

"Thirty-one days."

She walks toward you. Steady. Her boots on the stone. She stops at the edge of your boundary, the foot of cooler air the disc maintains, and you see her register it. Her eyes go to your chest. To the cord. To the disc.

"You have a ward."

"Gloria's father. Martin Aldrich."

"I know who Martin Aldrich is." She touches the edge of the cool zone. Her hand crosses into it and she breathes. A short, sharp inhale. "I've been reading his carvings for a month."

{has_mara_recording: Day eight. "The fear is not a byproduct. It's the product." You heard her voice on the recorder in Gloria's back room and now she's standing in front of you. The same person. The same voice. Thirty-one days past that recording and still working.}

{priority == "find_mara": You came to find her. Five days. Six nights. The motel room, the boo hole, the costume room, the tunnels, the library, the diner, the portal. All of it to get here. To this. Mara Castillo, alive, intact, standing in a space underneath the mine that doesn't end.}

{priority == "investigate_paranormal": She has thirty-one days of observations. Thirty-one days of documentation from the source side. Recordings, field notes, measurements. The investigation you were sent to conduct. She already ran it. She's been running it since the night she disappeared.}

"Tonight is the night," she says. "The cycles have been accelerating for three days. The compression rate is exponential. The system is approaching capacity." She looks at you. "You know what that means."

"The portal opens fully."

"The portal has always been open. Tonight it reaches saturation. Every person in that building, every guest, every scream. It's all going somewhere. Here. And when the input exceeds what the containment can hold..." She trails off. Looks at the amber haze. "This place expands. And what's above it contracts. Veinrock is inside the radius."

-> medallion_exchange


= medallion_exchange

She knows the symbol system. Thirty-one days of reading Martin Aldrich's carvings, of mapping the containment patterns from the source side, of understanding what the sigils do and how they fail and what would be required to restore them.

She knows how to seal it.

"The ward," she says. "The disc. It's the containment pattern. Cast in metal." She's not asking. She can see it. She's been reading the same language for a month. "If I can get it to the surface, to the carvings on the portal rim, the pattern can be completed. The disc is a key. Martin Aldrich made a key and wore it around his neck for thirty years."

She looks at the portal above you. The circle of amber light. The exhale pushes down in waves, and between waves the brief contraction, the pause, the second of stillness.

"I can't cross without it," she says. "There's no protection on this side. The passage strips you. Coming through is one direction. The portal pulls inward. Going back is against the current. Without the ward, the passage takes whatever it takes. I've been watching."

She has been watching. Thirty-one days of monitoring the portal from below. Watching the exhale cycles. Watching things come through that aren't people. Watching the portal breathe and knowing, with thirty-one days of evidence, exactly what it costs to go back through without protection.

You take the cord from around your neck.

The disc is warm in your hand. The vibration is steady, subtle, the counter-frequency that has kept this place from pressing in. You feel the boundary shift as you hold it away from your body. The cool zone retracts, tightens, follows the disc. The heat presses closer.

You hold it out to her.

{priority == "find_mara": You came to bring her back. This is the next step. She needs it. You give it to her. The arithmetic doesn't enter into it. Not yet.}

{priority == "investigate_paranormal": The documentation. Thirty-one days of source-side observations. Her notebooks, her recordings, her field measurements. All of it needs to reach the Order. She's the evidence. She needs to get out. The disc gets her out.}

Mara takes the disc. Her fingers close around it. The counter-frequency passes from your hand to hers and the boundary moves with it. The cool zone shifts, contracts around her body, and the air around you goes hot. Full heat. The weight of the source side pressing in from every direction without resistance.

She puts the cord around her neck.

"The containment carvings on the portal rim," she says. She's already moving. "The broken arcs. The disc completes the circuit. I can seal it from above."

She walks toward the portal. Toward the circle of light above. The disc hangs against her chest and the boundary of cool air moves with her and you watch her go.

She's ten feet away when you understand.

The heat. Your skin. The weight of this place against your body, against your lungs, against the backs of your eyes. The disc was the only thing between you and the full force of the source side. Without it, the passage back through the portal is not a passage. It's a wall.

You gave her the only protection you had. You did it because she needed it. You did it because the mission was to find her and the disc was the way to get her out and the rest of it, the part where you need it too, the part where without it you can't follow, that part didn't arrive until after your hand was empty.

Mara stops. She turns. She sees it in your face.

"Val."

"Go."

"I can't. The ward is the only..."

"I know. Go."

She stands there. The disc against her chest. The portal above. The exhale pushes down and the heat spikes and you both brace and hold through it. The pause.

Mara looks at you. Then she turns. She walks to the place beneath the portal. She looks up. She waits for the pause between breaths.

The exhale comes. Passes.

The pause.

Mara jumps.

The portal takes her. The amber light flares. The circle above swallows her silhouette and the disc's counter-frequency cuts through the portal's pull and for one second you can see her rising through the shaft, the ward holding, the containment symbol doing what Martin Aldrich built it to do.

She's through.

-> stranded


= stranded

The amber light settles. The exhale resumes. The rhythm of the source side: constant, patient, indifferent to who is or isn't here.

You're here.

The heat is total now. Not increasing. Just present. The full weight of the source side against your skin, your lungs, the fluid in your eyes. There is no boundary of cool air. No counter-frequency. No ward. Just you and the stone and the amber light and the hum that isn't a hum.

You sit down.

The stone is warm under you. Smooth. You put your hands flat on it. The vibration moves through your palms, up your wrists, into the bones of your arms. This place holds what it takes. That's what the source side does. Mara was here for thirty-one days and she was intact when you found her. The portal's surface effect, the dissolution, the thinning, the slow erasure that took your car and your name and the edges of your reflection, that happens above. Where the signal degrades over distance.

Here, at the source, the signal is clean. You're held. Preserved. Complete, legible, going nowhere.

You count the exhale cycles. This is the same skill. Groups in the boo hole: window bang, scream, reset, three minutes. The rhythm of the job, internalized. The rhythm of this place is different. Seventeen-second cycles, Mara said, when she arrived. Compressing. You count. Fourteen seconds. Thirteen.

Mara is working.

You know it because the cycles are compressing and the compression means the system is nearing capacity and the only thing that can stop it is the containment pattern being completed on the portal rim. And Mara has the disc. And Mara has thirty-one days of source-side analysis. And Mara has the complete symbol taxonomy and the map and the understanding of what every carved line means and how the circuit connects.

{has_mara_recording: Day eight. You found her. Got her out. The recorder is with her, the field notes are with her, thirty-one days of documentation are with her. The mission is complete. Not the way Silas briefed it. Not the way you planned it. But the agent is extracted. The evidence is topside. What happens to you is a separate line item.}

{priority == "find_mara": You came to bring Mara back. You brought her back. This is what that costs. You didn't calculate it when you held out the disc. You didn't weigh it. She needed the ward and you gave her the ward and the math arrived after your hand was empty. The mission was to find her. You found her. She's out.}

{priority == "investigate_paranormal": The documentation is with Mara. Everything she gathered in thirty-one days on the source side. Everything you gathered in six days on the surface. Her notebooks. Her recordings. Your field notes from the motel room, the library, the diner, the tunnels. The investigation continues. It just continues without you.}

{bex_relationship >= 5: Bex's mirror. The containment pattern traced in greasepaint across the glass. A signature. A map of what needed to happen, left in the material of the job, in the one place Bex worked every night. Mara knows the symbol system. She has the disc. She has Bex's work in the tunnels: decades of maintenance, of holding the circuit partway shut, of fading into the stone one line at a time. She has what she needs.}

You wait.

-> portal_sealing


= portal_sealing

The cycles change.

Twelve seconds. Then eleven. The exhale pushes down and the heat spikes and the pause between breaths gets shorter. Shorter. You count them because counting is what you do. What you did. The boo hole: three minutes between groups, hit the window, scream, reset. The seam: four minutes, grab, hold, release. The spiral steps: four seconds exhale, two seconds pause. Every position had a rhythm and you learned it and you performed it and the rhythm kept you whole.

Ten seconds.

Then something different. The exhale stutters. Not a clean breath. An interruption. A hitch. The rhythm that has been continuous since you arrived on the source side, the rhythm that predates the mine, the rhythm that has been breathing for longer than anything built above it. It falters.

Mara is working.

Nine seconds. Eight. The interruptions become more frequent. The exhale pushes, catches, resumes. Pushes, catches. The smooth cycle breaks into something irregular. The compression is still happening, the system approaching capacity, but something is interfering with the breathing. Something on the surface is closing the gaps in the circuit.

You stand.

The disc. Martin Aldrich wore it into the mine every shift for thirty years. Gloria kept it in a box in the back room of the diner for twenty-seven more. She gave it to you in the parking lot, late afternoon, sun going down. You gave it to Mara in the amber light of the source side. Mara is pressing it into the carvings on the portal rim right now. You can feel it.

The medallion changes hands. Gloria to you. You to Mara. Mara to the stone.

Martin Aldrich's protection passing through three people to reach the thing it was made for.

Seven seconds. Six.

The interruptions are constant now. Every exhale breaks. The rhythm of the source side, the deep, constant, patient breathing that is the sound this place makes, is being disrupted from above. The containment circuit closing, one completed arc at a time, the disc's frequency reinforcing the carved lines, the key fitting the lock.

Five.

Four.

The hum shifts. Not from above. From everywhere. The baseline frequency of the source side, the thing that isn't a hum because it's just the sound this place makes, changes pitch. Lower. Slower.

Three.

Two.

The hum stops.

Not fades. Stops. The silence is not the silence of a room where a sound has stopped. It's the silence of a space that is ceasing to be a space.

The amber light dims.

The stone under your feet is still warm. Still solid. But the warmth is draining. The light is draining. The vast chamber that extended in every direction without visible walls is contracting. Not closing in. Reducing. The space itself is becoming less.

You know it worked because you can feel yourself becoming less with it.

Not erasure. Not the slow thinning of the surface, the dissolution of memory and outline and name. This is different. This is the container being sealed with you inside. The source side held you and now the source side is being closed and what's inside goes with it.

Your hands. You look at your hands. They're there. Solid. No translucence. No blurring at the edges. The source side holds what it takes completely. You are complete. You are going to be complete right up until the moment there is no space left to be complete in.

The amber dims to a glow. To a suggestion. The stone cools under your feet.

Mara did it. The disc, the carvings, thirty-one days of analysis, fifty years of accumulated knowledge passing through Gloria's hands and Bex's hands and your hands into hers. The lock is closing. The lock is

-> DONE


= unprotected_crossing

You step off the edge.

No disc. No ward. No counter-frequency. Nothing between you and the passage but your body and the momentum of the step.

The crossing is not a crossing. It's a current. The portal pulls inward and you go with it and the passage is heat and pressure and a sound that isn't a sound and the amber light is everywhere and nowhere and your skin is not your skin and your

You land.

Stone. Hot stone. The source side. The vast amber chamber, the hum that isn't a hum, the heat. You're on your hands and knees. Your palms are flat on warm rock. You try to stand.

The pull is immediate. The portal's inward current doesn't stop at the threshold. On this side, without the ward, the current is constant. It pulls at you. Not a force you resist. A condition you exist inside. The source side is receiving you and the reception is not gentle.

{mara_clues >= 3:
    You see her.
    Across the chamber. A hundred feet. Two hundred. The scale is wrong and the distance is uncertain but she is there. Field clothes. Canvas jacket. Upright. Alive. Mara Castillo, standing in the amber light, and she sees you. She takes a step. You take a step. The distance between you doesn't change. The source side holds its distances absolutely.
    She reaches out. You reach out.
    Neither of you has the ward. Neither of you has the containment symbol, the counter-frequency, the protection that would let the passage go both ways. You are both here and here is closing. The portal above is reaching capacity and the source side is filling and the two of you are standing in it, arm's reach and a hundred feet apart, and the amber light is getting brighter.
    Mara's mouth moves. You can't hear her. The hum is too total. The sound this place makes swallows everything smaller than itself.
    She's been here thirty-one days. She's been working. Documenting. Waiting for extraction. And extraction arrived without the one thing that could make it work.
}

{mara_clues < 3:
    You search.
    The chamber extends in every direction. Amber light, sourceless, filling the space completely. The stone floor stretches into haze. You walk. You call out. Your voice doesn't carry. The hum absorbs it.
    The source side is vast. You could walk for hours. You could walk for days. Mara has been here for weeks and you don't know which direction she is and the space doesn't narrow and the light doesn't vary and every step feels like the first step.
    You keep walking. The pull keeps pulling. The heat keeps pressing.
    You can't find her.
}

The portal above flares. The exhale cycles compress. Twelve seconds. Ten. Eight. The system reaching capacity. The containment failing. The portal reaching saturation.

You followed her. You just couldn't bring either of you back.

Veinrock disappears tonight. The mine, the building, the guests, Gloria, Tuck. The portal reaches saturation and the source side expands and what's above it contracts into the blank spot on the map. The disc is in a box in the back room of a diner that will not exist by morning. The containment pattern is carved into stone that is being unmade from below.

Nobody seals the portal. Nobody gets out.

The amber light fills everything.

-> DONE
